    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a1a2d67_67d59a42",
        "filename": "xos/core/models/core.xproto",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 347,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000007
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-26T20:56:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "provider_interface and subscriber_interface fields are missing, I believe that the relation is implemented via the ManyToMany relation \"ServiceInterface\", why we need it to be a many to many relation? Do we expect a service to have multiple interfaces?\n\nI see that on the specs both are listed, we should decide which one we believe to be the correct implementation",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 334,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 347,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "9141248ca0acbd3dabc1d1920e4ddc5f51b8bf40",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a1a2d67_c7bd8e04",
        "filename": "xos/core/models/core.xproto",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 347,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000016
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-26T21:06:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, we expect that services may have multiple interfaces. VSG has two interfaces (lan and wan). It\u0027s conceivable that a service could have more than two interfaces. For example, one implmentation of monitoring could involve adding a monitoring interface to each service that wishes to participate in monitoring.\n\nThe interfaces are implemented in the ServiceInterface model. Xproto/django will automatically create the reverse relations, so Service will have a service_interfaces field provided automatically.",
      "parentUuid": "1a1a2d67_67d59a42",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 334,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 347,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "9141248ca0acbd3dabc1d1920e4ddc5f51b8bf40",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a1a2d67_47ca9ea2",
        "filename": "xos/core/models/core.xproto",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 480,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000007
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-26T20:56:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "subscriber_service_interface is missing, assuming that we want to replicate that information here. It may need some more discussion.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 480,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 480,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "9141248ca0acbd3dabc1d1920e4ddc5f51b8bf40",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a1a2d67_873796b0",
        "filename": "xos/core/models/core.xproto",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 480,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000016
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-26T21:06:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think it\u0027s necessary for a link to specify both the provider and subscriber service interfaces. The restriction is that they must match (i.e. you cannot connect a \"wan\" to a \"lan\"), so it\u0027s only necessary to specify one of the two, and then ensure that the other end of the link has a matching interface.",
      "parentUuid": "1a1a2d67_47ca9ea2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 480,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 480,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "9141248ca0acbd3dabc1d1920e4ddc5f51b8bf40",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a1a2d67_27cfa2b2",
        "filename": "xos/core/models/core.xproto",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 485,
